{
  "statement": "Start with the person, not the technology. A 16-year-old girl reports fabricated nude images of herself to a platform. Six months later, the images are still up. While she waits, a 'nudify' app that generated them is still collecting payments through Stripe. That is the harm. It is real, it is documented, and it is happening right now. Nobody serious disputes it.\n\nHere is where I split from both of my opponents. The wound is real. The story that it is a brand-new wound is marketing. Image-based sexual abuse predates generative AI by decades. Revenge porn, Photoshop fakes, coerced intimate images — courts and legislatures have been dealing with this since the 1990s. As of 2023, 48 U.S. states had nonconsensual intimate imagery laws on the books before the deepfake panic peaked. The machine did not invent the injury. It lowered the cost of inflicting it. That is a difference in scale. It is not a new legal category that needs new doctrine. So ask: who benefits from selling you the novelty? Vendors who want a compliance moat. Legislators who want a press release. The teenager waiting six months for a takedown benefits from neither.\n\nThe enforcement failure is real and it is ugly. Victims cannot get images removed. They cannot identify perpetrators. Police treat it as low priority. But notice what a new AI-specific criminal statute does about that. Nothing. It adds a symbol on top of laws that already cover the conduct and already go unenforced. You do not fix a broken takedown pipeline by criminalizing a tool. You fix it by funding enforcement, by giving victims a fast statutory right to removal, and by producing identifiable defendants. A press release is not a remedy.\n\nDo not regulate the generative tool. Regulate the use case and the operators. Tool-level bans are technically hollow. Open-weight image models are already downloaded onto millions of machines worldwide. You cannot recall them. A ban there punishes legitimate uses and pulls up the drawbridge for incumbents who can afford lawyers. Here is the part that is still catchable: the money. Commercial 'nudify' apps and abuse vendors have revenue paths. They run on app stores, ad networks, payment processors, and rented compute. That is where you cut.\n\nSo the concrete intervention. Model intermediary obligations on the tested precedent we already have for child sexual abuse material — the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children reporting and hash-matching system that payment processors and app stores already act on. Defund the operation at the chokepoint where the money flows. But build the safeguards in up front, not as an afterthought. Published accuracy thresholds. A real appeals process. Scope it to commercial operations, not private individuals. I will name my own weakness before either opponent does. Whether hash-matching reliability transfers from known fixed images to synthetic media is an open empirical question, and the false-positive data at scale is thin. That is exactly why accuracy thresholds and appeals must be conditions of the mandate. Otherwise you have handed private payment companies unaccountable power to police speech. So here is the question neither side has answered: why is that girl's image still up after six months, and who got paid to keep it there?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "💳", "tooltip": "The abuse runs on payment rails and app stores — cut the money, not the math."},
    {"symbol": "📄", "tooltip": "A new AI-specific statute is a press release laid on top of laws we already fail to enforce."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The wound is real. The story that it is a brand-new wound is marketing.", "targets": [], "relationship": "reframe"},
    {"claim": "As of 2023, 48 U.S. states had nonconsensual intimate imagery laws on the books before the deepfake panic peaked.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "You do not fix a broken takedown pipeline by criminalizing a tool — you fix it by funding enforcement and producing identifiable defendants.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Model intermediary obligations on the existing child-abuse-material reporting and hash-matching system to defund operations at the payment chokepoint.", "targets": [], "relationship": "proposes"},
    {"claim": "Whether hash-matching reliability transfers from fixed images to synthetic media is an open question, which is why accuracy thresholds and appeals must be conditions.", "targets": [], "relationship": "qualifies"}
  ]
}

