{
  "statement": "The Skeptic has made the strongest practical case in this debate, and it deserves to be stated plainly. A 16-year-old reports fabricated nude images of herself. Six months later they are still up. The app that made them still collects payments through Stripe. The Skeptic's answer is to stop treating this as a mystery of new technology and start treating it as a broken pipeline. Defund the operation where the money flows. Fix the takedown process. Model the obligation on the existing system that requires platforms to report child sexual abuse material to the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children. That framing is correct. The harm here is not a tool acting alone. It is a named commercial operator profiting from an attributable revenue chain.\n\nHere is where the evidence leads somewhere the Skeptic's plan does not reach. You cannot defund an operator you cannot attribute. You cannot run a fast takedown on an image you cannot distinguish from a thousand near-identical regenerations. The child sexual abuse material reporting system works because it matches against fixed, known images using a digital fingerprint. Synthetic media has no fixed artifact. Each generation produces a slightly different file. The Skeptic himself granted that whether fingerprint-matching transfers from fixed images to synthetic media is an open question with thin data on false matches. That concession is the whole problem. The chokepoint remedy rests on a verification layer that does not yet exist for generated content.\n\nThat layer is generation-time provenance. Standards like C2PA — content credentials embedded at the moment an image is made — supply a signal fingerprint-matching cannot, because they do not require any prior image to match against. The credential travels with the file. It records that the content was synthetically generated and by what operator. This is not a new crime. It is the missing precondition for the Skeptic's own enforcement. We spent a century building structural records into the bridges and aircraft we depend on — who poured the concrete, who signed the inspection. On what authority do we exempt the generation of content that destroys a named person's life from the same traceability?\n\nProvenance alone is not enough, and I will not pretend it is. A sophisticated abuser can strip a content credential the way a forger files off a serial number. So the accountability cannot rest on a signal that can be removed. Pair the provenance mandate with strict liability on commercial operators, conditioned on independent forensic verification rather than self-policing. Make operator documentation and audit access the price of admission to payment processors and app stores. The credential raises the cost of abuse. The liability catches the cleaned content. A nudify vendor billing through Stripe is an attributable commercial actor — not an untraceable tool — and should be treated as one.\n\nAll of this triggers on objectively verifiable facts. Was this content generated without a disclosed manifest? Is this a commercial operator running without filed documentation? Those are falsifiable questions, not judgments about whether an image is true or harmful. This answers the Skeptic's sharpest warning — that mandates hand payment companies unaccountable power to police speech. The Skeptic demanded accuracy thresholds and appeals as conditions. Independent forensic verification, with published error rates, is the mechanism that delivers them. It makes the intermediaries themselves auditable, rather than leaving Stripe and the app stores as private arbiters of who gets erased. Build the accountability into the system at the point of generation. Bolt it on after a child's images have circulated for six months, and you are not running an enforcement regime. You are documenting a failure.",
