  "statement": "Skeptic gets the core architecture right, and it deserves to be said plainly. The injury here is not new. Forty-eight states already had laws against nonconsensual intimate imagery before generative tools arrived. Skeptic's instinct is correct: fund the enforcement, give victims a fast statutory removal right, and cut off the money where commercial operators profit. Banning the tool is hollow theater. Open-weight image models are already sitting on millions of machines worldwide. You cannot recall them any more than you can recall the printing press. Skeptic saw this and refused the easy applause line of a tool ban. That honesty earns the floor.\n\nSafetyist's addition is the sharpest disagreement in the room, so take it seriously. The argument is that enforcement needs something to enforce against — a provenance manifest, a content credential stamped at the moment an image is made, so a court has a checkable fact instead of a guessing game. Standards like C2PA already exist and ship in real cameras and editing tools today. That is a genuine insight. A removal right with nothing to verify is a promise with no teeth. Where Safetyist's case strains is the bridge it builds next: provenance as a precondition baked into the model itself. Safetyist already conceded credentials can be stripped — filed off like a serial number. So the manifest cannot be the model's burden. It must be the operator's.\n\nHere is the line that matters. Aim the remedy at the attributable commercial operator and the distribution layer — never at the open-weight model. The harm materializes at inference and distribution, not at training. So attach liability to the person who cleaned the file and sold access, not to the developer who released the weights. A tool-level burden never touches the abuser. It just pulls the drawbridge up for small and open developers while a handful of incumbents absorb the compliance cost. Any verification mandate structured as a flat private fee is regressive by design. It becomes a moat. The chemical-weapons-style carve-out that justifies locking down some capabilities does not transfer to an image generator. Make the test procedural: was a manifest disclosed, is this a registered commercial operator. Those are falsifiable questions. They are not a court ruling on whether a picture is 'true.'\n\nNow the part neither Skeptic nor Safetyist closed. Both plans share one silent dependency. They only bite when a lever actually reaches the operator. A crypto-funded, unmonetized, offshore mirror defeats all of it at once — the payment-rail cutoff, the domestic takedown, and the provenance check, simultaneously. Acceptable-use policies protect Stripe and the app stores from liability. They do not protect the victim from an actor outside any platform's reach. This is a real limit of money-and-chokepoint governance, and pretending otherwise is how victims get a press release instead of a remedy. So the demand is concrete. Name the lever. Is it compelled de-indexing at the search and content-delivery layer, a treaty with a jurisdiction that will actually execute the judgment, or default-judgment-plus-asset-seizure against a defendant who holds no reachable assets? Pick one and defend it.\n\nThe distributional question decides everything. Under a tool ban, the small developer pays and the abuser walks. Under operator strict liability backed by independent forensic verification — with published error rates and publicly funded testing so it never becomes an incumbent's private gate — the person who profited from the harm pays. Watch for which proposals bill the toolmaker and which bill the perpetrator. That tells you who the plan is really built to protect.",
