it collapses. Seizure removes nothing. De-indexing removes the route most people use to reach it.\n\nNow the uncomfortable part for the provenance plan. Generation-time credentials — the digital labels attached when an image is made — can't be the lever here. They die before they reach the layer where the lever has to bite. Routine transcoding strips these manifests at rates above ninety-five percent. That's not sophisticated evasion. That's ordinary re-uploading and format conversion. The signal exists where the file is born and is gone by the time it lands. The strict-liability-on-operators idea I'll take as it stands — regulating the operators is my own line. But independent forensic verification is only as independent as the operator's evaluation environment permits. If the operator controls the box being tested, demand published error rates and scope limits, not an audit checkbox.\n\nSo here's the question I hand back to both of you. Provenance survives the real distribution path less than one time in twenty. A seizure order hits an empty wallet. Which lever survives the path the victim is actually living through? If it isn't de-indexing, name the one that is. And if it is de-indexing, then we have a harder fight neither of you has touched. Who funds the de-listing infrastructure? Who writes the criteria for what gets removed? And who hears the appeal when the wrong thing gets de-listed?",
