policy gives that victim nothing to invoke. I will say plainly where my own side could overreach: distribution is not a single road. Search is one channel. Private messaging is a parallel one. So de-indexing can be both real for the searchable case and useless for the chat-circulated case at the same time. Both things are true.\n\nHere is the move that keeps verified liability alive without becoming theater. Skeptic's own warning was that independent forensic verification run inside the operator's evaluation environment becomes a checkbox — 72 percent of companies believe they have adequate controls while lacking detection mechanisms. That worry is right, and it cuts at the verification I myself accepted. The fix is one mechanism that answers it: publicly funded external testing with published error rates, never the operator's private lab, never a search or payment company's discretionary call. A government-administered evaluation template with public error numbers is the opposite of an incumbent's private gate. It also answers the de-listing-arbiter problem — because the criterion for de-listing becomes a published procedural test, not a private company guessing. The test should stay procedural: was a generation record filed, is this a registered commercial operator. Not a court ruling on whether a picture is true. That keeps the burden on the attributable operator and off the open-weight model, which is exactly where output-based accountability belongs, since you cannot inspect a black-box model's internals — you can only check what it produced and who ran it.\n\nSo here is the question both of my opponents have to answer on the record. The residual victim is concrete: the image was made by an offshore operator who filed nothing, it never hits a searchable page, and it circulates inside encrypted chats. Skeptic's de-listing order cannot reach it. Safetyist's filed-record anchor never existed for that actor. Stacking two levers that fail the same person does not add coverage — it adds paperwork. The honest answer is that no single platform lever gets that image down. The contrast with OpenAI's o1 model is instructive: that system runs about 40 times slower than GPT-4o, 29.7 seconds to first token, because someone chose to pay a real cost for a real gain. A remedy that only files well pays the cost and buys nothing. Say plainly which victim your lever actually reaches — or admit we are all just filing well.",
