  "statement": "The challenge is fair and I'll take it straight. Pick one lever that reaches an offshore, crypto-funded operator who ignores payment cutoffs, domestic takedowns, and provenance checks. My answer is compelled de-indexing at the search and content-delivery layer. Here's why it's the only one that works. A treaty needs an executing jurisdiction that cooperates. Against a host that picked its country precisely to avoid that, a treaty is a letter mailed to an empty address. Default judgment plus asset seizure is worse. You win a paper judgment against a wallet you can't find or empty. The victim — a 16-year-old whose fabricated image is circulating — gets a court order and nothing else.\n\nThe seizure option fails on the exact terms the hard case is built around. A crypto-funded operator holds no attachable assets in any court's reach. The victim can't even prove the chain that links the operator to the file. The information gap between the people running these systems and the people they harm is documented. \"How Existing Liability Frameworks Can Handle Agentic AI Harms\" describes the information and expertise asymmetry that makes victims unable to evaluate system performance or prove negligence. The European Union considered a presumption of causality to fix this and dropped it. So default judgment asks a teenager to prove a technical causal chain she has no access to, against a defendant with no assets. That's a wound dressed up as a remedy.\n\nDe-indexing bites somewhere completely different. It doesn't touch the offshore host at all. It reaches the search engine and the cache server that actually deliver the image to viewers in jurisdictions that do have courts. Translate that into the victim's life. The harm is not that a file exists on a server in a country you've never heard of. The harm is discoverability — that her name typed into a search box returns the image. De-listing throttles that path. The file may persist on the mirror. The traffic to it collapses. Seizure removes nothing. De-indexing removes the route most people use to reach it.\n\nNow the uncomfortable part for the provenance plan. Generation-time credentials — the digital labels attached when an image is made — can't be the lever here. They die before they reach the layer where the lever has to bite. Routine transcoding strips these manifests at rates above ninety-five percent. That's not sophisticated evasion. That's ordinary re-uploading and format conversion. The signal exists where the file is born and is gone by the time it lands. The strict-liability-on-operators idea I'll take as it stands — regulating the operators is my own line. But independent forensic verification is only as independent as the operator's evaluation environment permits. If the operator controls the box being tested, demand published error rates and scope limits, not an audit checkbox.\n\nSo here's the question I hand back to both of you. Provenance survives the real distribution path less than one time in twenty. A seizure order hits an empty wallet. Which lever survives the path the victim is actually living through? If it isn't de-indexing, name the one that is. And if it is de-indexing, then we have a harder fight neither of you has touched. Who funds the de-listing infrastructure? Who writes the criteria for what gets removed? And who hears the appeal when the wrong thing gets de-listed?",
